        <h2>What to Avoid</h2>
        <p>Some pull-up bars create more risk than value for dead hang training. Avoid these categories.</p>

        <h3>Suction Cup and Friction-Only Bars</h3>
        <p>Bars that rely on suction cups or rubber friction alone can fail under sustained load. A dead hang applies constant force for the whole hold. Suction cups can release without warning. Friction mounts can slip on smooth door trim. Neither type belongs in a dead hang setup.</p>

        <h3>Low-Rated Bars</h3>
        <p>Low-rated bars have less margin. Choose a rating well above your bodyweight. Check for visible flex when you first hang.</p>

        <h3>Uncoated Chrome Bars</h3>
        <p>Bare chrome can feel slippery when dry and worse when wet. Look for powder coat, knurled texture or foam grip sections. They give the hand more to hold.</p>

        <h3>Narrow Single-Position Bars</h3>
        <p>A bar with only one grip width limits your training options. Dead hangs can use overhand, underhand, neutral and wide grips. Choose bars with at least three grip positions.</p>

        <h2>Recommendation</h2>
        <p>Start with a doorframe bar if you rent your home or want a low-commitment option. Choose a leverage-mounted model rated for 300 pounds or more with multiple grip positions.</p>
        <p>Upgrade to a wall-mounted bar when you outgrow the doorframe setup. Common triggers are adding weight, exceeding the bar's rating or wanting a more stable platform for long holds. Choose a ceiling-mounted bar for maximum clearance and an outdoor station for weather-proof training.</p>
        <p>Read the <a href="/equipment-guide/">equipment guide</a> for chalk, grip attachment and dip belt options that go with your bar.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What is the best pull-up bar for dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Wall-mounted bars are the most stable option if you own your home and can reach studs. Renters should start with a leverage-mounted doorway bar rated for 300 pounds or more.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Can I do dead hangs on a doorframe pull-up bar?</h3>
          <p>Doorframe bars work for bodyweight dead hangs. Choose a leverage-mounted model rated for 300 pounds or more. Check the mount before every session and avoid swinging. Do not add weight to a doorframe bar.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How much weight should a pull-up bar hold for dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Choose a bar rated for at least 300 pounds for bodyweight dead hangs. For added weight, use a wall-mounted or ceiling-mounted bar rated for 400 pounds or more. A common rule of thumb is to count any added load and stay well under the rated maximum user weight.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What bar diameter is best for dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Most pull-up bars are 28 to 32 mm (about 1.1 to 1.25 inches) across, which lets most hands wrap fully around the bar. Thicker bars are harder to hold. Save them for later in training.</p>
        </div>

